\title{Chinese Remainder Theorem}
\date{2026-07-16T21:31:31Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{For all #{a,b \in \Z}, if #{\gcd(m,n) = 1} then the system of equivalences ##{\begin{align*}
    x &\equiv_m a \\
    x &\equiv_n b
\end{align*}} has a unique solution modulo #{mn}.}

\proof{
  Since #{\gcd(m,n) = 1}, [[00FG]] tells us that there exist integers #{s} and #{t} such that #{sm + tn = 1} (and we can compute them using the [extended Euclidean algorithm](00FH)).  Then we claim that ##{x \equiv_{mn} atn + bsm} is a solution to the system of equivalences for #{x}.  First, to see that #{atn + bsm \equiv_m a}, we reason as follows:
\stmt{atn + bsm}
\reason{=}{#{sm + tn = 1}; substitute #{1 - sm} for #{tn}}
\stmt{a(1 - sm) + bsm}
\reason{=}{expand}
\stmt{a - asm + bsm}
\reason{=}{factor out #{sm}}
\stmt{a + (b - a)sm}
\reason{\equiv_m}{#{a} plus a multiple of #{m}}
\stmt{a}

The proof that #{atn + bsm \equiv_n b} is similar.
}
